nd_preimage_f5155341_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the preimage $\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})$ of a set $\\boldsymbol{V}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{Y}$?","options":["The set of outputs outside $\\boldsymbol{V}$.","The set of inputs $\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\boldsymbol{X}$ such that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})\\in\\boldsymbol{V}$.","Only the points of $\\boldsymbol{V}$ that have unique inputs.","A function that must undo $\\boldsymbol{f}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0df36222473c2d507e2c72f9"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x,y) = x+y$. What is the image of $[0,1]\\times[0,1]$?","options":["$[-1,1]$","$[0,2]$","$\\{0\\}$","$[2,3]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"59d921d4815caa84b39200fa"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x+y$, what is the preimage of $\\{0\\}$?","options":["The square $[0,1]\\times[0,1]$.","The line $y = -x$.","The interval $[0,2]$.","The vertical strip $[2,3]\\times\\mathbb{R}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e815619fa5120efb7fabfa3"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which inclusion is stated for a subset $\\boldsymbol{C}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{X}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{C}))\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{C}$ always","$\\boldsymbol{C} = \\boldsymbol{Y}$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{C})\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{X}$","$\\boldsymbol{C}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{C}))$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cb91006760b1d2c5c6fd9fb2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which inclusion is stated for a subset $\\boldsymbol{V}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{Y}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{V}\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V}))$ always","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V}))\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{V}$","$\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})\\subseteq\\boldsymbol{Y}$","$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{V}) = \\boldsymbol{X}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e267132f85883a3e6edc8fd"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with $f(x,y) = x$, what is the image of the disc $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon x^2+y^2\\ \\leq 1\\}$?","options":["$[-1,1]$","$[0,2]$","$[2,3]$","$\\mathbb{R}^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d7986250a76f34fe291dd72"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with $f(x,y) = x$, what is $f^{-1}([2,3])$?","options":["$[2,3]\\times\\mathbb{R}$","$[-1,1]$","$[0,1]\\times[0,1]$","$\\{(x,y)\\colon y = -x\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1912f0d2dceb900a55b135e0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why may $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V}))$ be a proper subset of $\\boldsymbol{V}$?","options":["The preimage is always empty.","The set $\\boldsymbol{V}$ must lie in the domain.","Some points of $\\boldsymbol{V}$ may not be hit by any input of $\\boldsymbol{f}$.","Images are always larger than their target sets."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fe4f52dd8c632158a870fe67"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the notation $\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})$ not require $\\boldsymbol{f}$ to have an inverse function?","options":["It is defined only when $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is bijective, no matter which values are selected.","It means dividing by $\\boldsymbol{f}$.","It denotes a set of inputs mapped into $\\boldsymbol{V}$, not necessarily an inverse function value.","It is always a subset of the codomain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5241981e2cd8fb994d4f57e"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x,y) = x+y$ and $\\boldsymbol{C} = \\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\\}$. What is $f(\\boldsymbol{C})$?","options":["$\\{0,1\\}$","$\\{0,1,2\\}$","$\\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\\}$","$[0,2]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"156c877d4a5dc0d6748bb112"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x,y) = x$. Which point lies in $f^{-1}([2,3])$?","options":["$(2.5,-7)$","$(1,2)$","$(4,0)$","$(0,2.5)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e56c75bdc853a2c9c71377dc"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of elements does $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{C})$ contain?","options":["Outputs from inputs in $\\boldsymbol{C}$.","All inputs from the codomain.","Only points outside the range.","All subsets of the domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c1a076daf885c5cc9a0f7505"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of elements does $\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})$ contain?","options":["Outputs that are not attained.","Only inverse-function values.","Inputs whose outputs lie in $\\boldsymbol{V}$.","Only points in the codomain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92cd695629b26e9217c45a98"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is $\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})$ necessarily a subset of the domain or the codomain?","options":["A subset of the codomain.","A subset of the domain.","A subset of both in every case.","Neither; it is always a number."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8d616f8d6d0284090b0a5b85"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x)=x^2$. What is $f(\\{-1,0,1\\})$?","options":["$\\{-1,0,1\\}$","$\\{0,1\\}$","$\\{1\\}$","$[0,1]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ecd4dd1ab6d8d354bf8f05e2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x)=x^2$. What is $f^{-1}(\\{4\\})$?","options":["$\\{4\\}$","$\\{2\\}$","$\\{-2,2\\}$","$\\varnothing$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0650d4c31025d621009b7c7a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x)=x+1$. What is $f^{-1}([1,3])$?","options":["$[1,3]$","$[2,4]$","$[0,2]$","$[-1,1]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca51528a2f409b73b6d818d2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the notation $\\boldsymbol{f}^{-1}(\\boldsymbol{V})$ not require an inverse function?","options":["It always denotes a single inverse value.","It is defined only when $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is bijective.","It denotes a set of inputs mapping into $\\boldsymbol{V}$.","It is the reciprocal of $\\boldsymbol{f}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"edb39973e4c41d51e5d4c1e3"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_image_and_preimage_f5155341_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x,y)=x-y$. Which point lies in $f^{-1}(\\{0\\})$?","options":["$(2,1)$","$(0,1)$","$(2,2)$","$(3,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a6ef0d69b0c191a8491cb2dc"}]}}